Others’ words, and pointers at sources — quotation, citation, cross-reference, and links.

<section #sh-a | `<a>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *a*. Compiles to eHTML `<a>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<a>`

<code #code:sh-a-1>
See <a https://example.com/docs | the documentation>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-1>
See <a https://example.com/docs | the documentation>.
</minipage>

*The positional URL form — the target is the first argument, the pipe
content is the link text. The most common authoring path. (Markdown
`[text](url)` is not an enscribe idiom; it renders as literal text.)*

<code #code:sh-a-2>
<a href=https://example.com | the example site>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-2>
<a href=https://example.com | the example site>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-a-3>
<a href=https://example.com target=_blank rel=noopener | external link>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-3>
<a href=https://example.com target=_blank rel=noopener | external link>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:sh-a-4>
<a href=#section-2 | jump to Section 2>

<section #section-2 | Section 2>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-4>
<a href=#section-2 | jump to Section 2>

<section #section-2 | Section 2>
</minipage>

*Internal links use fragment identifiers pointing at element ids — here
the in-scope &lt;section #section-2&gt; the link targets.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `href` | format-word |  | The link target as the first positional argument: |
| `href` | kwarg |  | The URL or fragment identifier the anchor points to. |
| `target` | kwarg | `_self` `_blank` `_parent` `_top` | How the link opens. |
| `rel` | kwarg |  | Relationship between the current document and the link target. |
| `title` | kwarg |  | Tooltip text shown on hover. |

<section #sh-bib-entry | `<bib-entry>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *bib-entry*. Compiles to eHTML `<bib-entry>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<bib-entry>`

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `article` `book` `chapter` `thesis` `proceedings` `report` `webpage` `other` | The bibliography entry type. |

<section #sh-bibliography | `<bibliography>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *bibliography*. Compiles to eHTML `<bibliography>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<bibliography>`

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `style` | kwarg | `author-year` `numbered` `footnote` `custom` | Future — not yet implemented: |
| `sort` | kwarg | `alpha` `citation-order` `year` `none` (default `alpha`) | Future — not yet implemented: |
| `type` | kwarg | `cited-only` `full` `hybrid` (default `cited-only`) | Future — not yet implemented: |

<section #sh-blockquote | `<blockquote>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *blockquote*. Compiles to eHTML `<blockquote>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<blockquote>`
- Alias: `<quote>`

<code #code:sh-blockquote-1>
<quote | A short quotation.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-blockquote-1>
<quote | A short quotation.>
</minipage>

*The &lt;quote&gt; shorthand expands to &lt;blockquote&gt; at eHTML.*

<code-block #code:sh-blockquote-2>
<quote cite=https://example.com/source |
A longer quotation that may contain multiple paragraphs.

The second paragraph of the quotation.
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-blockquote-2>
<quote cite=https://example.com/source |
A longer quotation that may contain multiple paragraphs.

The second paragraph of the quotation.
>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:sh-blockquote-3>
<quote type=epigraph |
All happy families are alike; each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-blockquote-3>
<quote type=epigraph |
All happy families are alike; each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.
>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-blockquote-4>
<blockquote | Same as `<quote>` but using the explicit eHTML name.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-blockquote-4>
<blockquote | Same as `<quote>` but using the explicit eHTML name.>
</minipage>

*Authors can also write &lt;blockquote&gt; directly. Both forms produce
the same eHTML output.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cite` | kwarg |  | URL of the source being quoted. |
| `type` | kwarg | `verse` `dialogue` `epigraph` `pullquote` `other` | Optional classification of the quotation's role. |

<section #sh-cite | `<cite>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *cite*. Compiles to eHTML `<cite>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<cite>`

<code #code:sh-cite-1>
The argument is supported by data <cite goodall2024>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-cite-1>
The argument is supported by data <cite goodall2024>.
</minipage>

*A simple citation. The resolver renders the citation according
to the document-level style. For author-year style, this becomes
"(Goodall 2024)". For numbered style, "[1]". The actual rendered
text is generated by the citation resolver, not written by the author.*

<code #code:sh-cite-2>
Multiple sources confirm this <cite goodall2024 attenborough2020 darwin1859>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-cite-2>
Multiple sources confirm this <cite goodall2024 attenborough2020 darwin1859>.
</minipage>

*Multiple citation keys group into one citation marker. The resolver
handles the joining (with separators appropriate to the style).*

<code #code:sh-cite-3>
See <cite @goodall2024, p. 42> for details.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-cite-3>
See <cite @goodall2024, p. 42> for details.
</minipage>

*A citation with a page locator (the #409 item grammar; the kwarg long
form `&lt;cite @goodall2024 page=42&gt;` renders identically). The locator
flows through the CSL style, which renders alongside the
citation marker.*

<code #code:sh-cite-4>
See also <cite cf. @goodall2024, pp. 42-45>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-cite-4>
See also <cite cf. @goodall2024, pp. 42-45>.
</minipage>

*Citation with a positional prefix and a page range (Pandoc grammar).*

<code #code:sh-cite-5>
As <cite style=narrative @goodall2024> showed, the effect holds.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-cite-5>
As <cite style=narrative @goodall2024> showed, the effect holds.
</minipage>

*The narrative form (#418) — author-in-text, LaTeX \citet, APA's
"narrative citation". The author name joins the sentence; the year
stays parenthetical. Composed from citeproc's author-only +
suppress-author parts, so the CSL style still owns punctuation.*

<code #code:sh-cite-6>
Goodall showed this in <cite style=suppress-author @goodall2024>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-cite-6>
Goodall showed this in <cite style=suppress-author @goodall2024>.
</minipage>

*The suppress-author form (#418) — the year alone, for when the
author is already named in the prose. Zotero's Suppress Author,
Pandoc's [-@key], citeproc's own per-item flag.*

<code #code:sh-cite-7>
As <cite style=narrative @goodall2024, p. 42> notes, context matters.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-cite-7>
As <cite style=narrative @goodall2024, p. 42> notes, context matters.
</minipage>

*A form composes with a #409 locator: the page rides the
parenthetical part of the narrative composite.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `keys` | kwarg |  | One or more citation keys, supplied as positional arguments. |
| `page` | kwarg |  | Page number or range — the kwarg long form of the locator (#409), the escape hatch for values the inline grammar cannot express (page="iv, vi-xi"). |
| `chapter` | kwarg |  | Chapter reference within the cited work. |
| `section` | kwarg |  | Section reference within the cited work. |
| `prefix` | kwarg |  | Text to appear before the citation. |
| `suffix` | kwarg |  | Text to appear after the citation. |
| `style` | kwarg | `parenthetical` `narrative` `suppress-author` (default `parenthetical`) | The per-citation FORM (#418) — the rendering mode of the one document-wide citation style, never a per-cite CSL-style switch (the document has ONE style; the References list stays coherent). |

<section #sh-doi | `<doi>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *doi*. Compiles to eHTML `<doi>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<doi>`

<code-block #code:sh-doi-1>
<meta>
  <doi | 10.1234/example.2024>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-doi-1>
<meta>
  <doi | 10.1234/example.2024>
</meta>
</minipage>

*Bare DOI as the typical authoring form.*

<code #code:sh-doi-2>
<meta doi="10.5555/test" />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-doi-2>
<meta doi="10.5555/test" />
</minipage>

*Kwarg-form authoring lifts to the child-tag form at the
normalize-to-canonical gate (per the apparatus-tag reconciliation,
DESIGN.md §"Apparatus-tag positioning").*

<section #sh-q | `<q>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *q*. Compiles to eHTML `<q>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<q>`

<code #code:sh-q-1>
She said <q | hello> in passing.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-q-1>
She said <q | hello> in passing.
</minipage>

*Browsers automatically render &lt;q&gt; with quotation marks. Authors
do not include quotation marks in the content.*

<code #code:sh-q-2>
The phrase <q cite=https://example.com | to be or not to be> is iconic.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-q-2>
The phrase <q cite=https://example.com | to be or not to be> is iconic.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cite` | kwarg |  | URL of the source being quoted. |

<section #sh-ref | `<ref>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *ref*. Compiles to eHTML `<ref>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<ref>`

<code-block #code:sh-ref-1>
<fig #fig:scatter | A scatter plot of the results.>
See <ref @fig:scatter> for details.
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-ref-1>
<fig #fig:scatter | A scatter plot of the results.>
See <ref @fig:scatter> for details.
</minipage>

*Canonical form: @id prefix (@ references; # would declare an id on the
&lt;ref&gt; itself). A &lt;ref&gt; resolves only against a target in scope, so the
demo declares &lt;fig #fig:scatter&gt; first; the ref-resolution plugin then
replaces the &lt;ref&gt; node with a __ref-marker before hast conversion, and
the handler renders an &lt;a&gt; whose text ("figure 1") is computed from the
target's kind and number. Prefix word is lowercase, from the
DEFAULT_PREFIXES dictionary keyed by id prefix.*

<code-block #code:sh-ref-2>
<$$ #eqn:model | y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x $$>
As shown in <ref @eqn:model>.
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-ref-2>
<$$ #eqn:model | y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x $$>
As shown in <ref @eqn:model>.
</minipage>

*Equation references use the "equation" prefix word by default; the
display equation &lt;$$ #eqn:model … $$&gt; supplies the in-scope target.
Config override: &lt;config ref-prefix-eqn="Eq."&gt; changes this to "Eq.".*

<code #code:sh-ref-3>
<ref @eqn:missing>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-ref-3>
<ref @eqn:missing>
</minipage>

*Unresolved target renders a visible error anchor. This example is the
deliberate exception — it teaches the error form on purpose.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `target` | kwarg |  | The id of the element being referenced. |
| `type` | kwarg | `auto` `figure` `table` `equation` `section` `sub-section` `note` `listing` `theorem` `other` (default `auto`) | Author-supplied hint about the intended kind of target. |
| `format` | kwarg | `number` `name` `full` `label-only` `default` (default `default`) | Author-supplied formatting hint. |
| `+link` / `-link` | flag | default on | Controls whether the rendered ref is a navigable hyperlink. |
| `+preview` / `-preview` | flag | default on | Controls the hover preview that attaches to the rendered ref by default. |
| `+title` / `-title` | flag | default on | Reserved / unimplemented. |

<data>
<library bibtex>
@book{goodall2024,   author={Goodall, Jane},    title={Reflections on Primatology}, publisher={Field Press},   year={2024}}
@article{smith2023,  author={Smith, Ann},       title={Structured Documents},       journal={J. Doc. Eng.},    year={2023}}
@book{jones2024,     author={Jones, Bo},        title={The Citation Habit},         publisher={Scholars Row},  year={2024}}
@book{attenborough2020, author={Attenborough, David}, title={A Life on Our Planet}, publisher={Witness Books}, year={2020}}
@book{darwin1859,    author={Darwin, Charles},   title={On the Origin of Species},   publisher={John Murray},   year={1859}}
</library>
</data>
